WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.225
Lesson 1.4.

00:00:02.225 --> 00:00:04.735
Tokens, approvals and allowances.

00:00:04.735 --> 00:00:06.312
By the end, you'll be able to

00:00:06.312 --> 00:00:08.520
read an approval request and know exactly

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.675
what you're allowing, before you sign it.

00:00:11.975 --> 00:00:13.265
Here's the plan.

00:00:13.265 --> 00:00:16.020
First, what tokens actually are, and why a name

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.730
can be faked but a contract address can't.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.337
Second, approvals and allowances, and why

00:00:21.337 --> 00:00:23.375
unlimited ones are the trap.

00:00:23.375 --> 00:00:25.826
Third, the gasless signature that's even

00:00:25.826 --> 00:00:27.680
easier to sign by mistake.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.513
And finally, a full worked example: reading

00:00:30.513 --> 00:00:32.960
a real approval request, step by step.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.805
What a token actually is.

00:00:37.105 --> 00:00:41.499
Most tokens on Ethereum and similar chains follow

00:00:41.499 --> 00:00:46.333
a standard called ERC-20: fungible tokens, where every

00:00:46.333 --> 00:00:50.940
unit is interchangeable, like USDC or ETH itself.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:56.429
NFTs, following ERC-721 or ERC-1155, are non-fungible: each one

00:00:56.429 --> 00:01:00.888
is unique, and in DeFi they can represent something

00:01:00.888 --> 00:01:06.205
like a specific concentrated liquidity position, not just art.

00:01:07.505 --> 00:01:10.004
Here's how a fake token actually gets in front

00:01:10.004 --> 00:01:12.803
of you, since it's more mundane than it sounds.

00:01:12.803 --> 00:01:15.259
Someone deploys a token, names it and

00:01:15.259 --> 00:01:17.714
its ticker identically to a real one,

00:01:17.714 --> 00:01:20.428
and adds it to a decentralised exchange's

00:01:20.428 --> 00:01:23.701
listing, or airdrops it directly into wallets.

00:01:23.701 --> 00:01:26.500
Your wallet app might even display it with the

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.895
right name and logo, pulled automatically from that fake

00:01:29.895 --> 00:01:33.529
contract's own metadata, because the display name is exactly

00:01:33.529 --> 00:01:35.911
as fakeable as everything else about it.

00:01:37.211 --> 00:01:40.156
Here's the fact that matters more than either standard.

00:01:40.156 --> 00:01:42.523
Anyone can create a token and call it

00:01:42.523 --> 00:01:45.201
“USDC.” The name and the ticker symbol are

00:01:45.201 --> 00:01:48.254
just text, chosen by whoever deployed the token,

00:01:48.254 --> 00:01:50.796
and they're completely free to fake.

00:01:50.796 --> 00:01:53.912
The only thing that actually identifies a token

00:01:53.912 --> 00:01:56.834
is its contract address, a fixed string that

00:01:56.834 --> 00:01:59.366
only the real, original deployment has.

00:02:00.666 --> 00:02:03.457
One concrete example of an NFT as a

00:02:03.457 --> 00:02:07.954
DeFi position, not art: a concentrated liquidity position

00:02:07.954 --> 00:02:11.211
on an exchange like Uniswap can itself be

00:02:11.211 --> 00:02:14.778
an NFT. Its metadata encodes your price range

00:02:14.778 --> 00:02:17.171
and your share of the pool.

00:02:17.171 --> 00:02:20.570
You'll price and manage exactly this kind of position

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:23.655
properly in Module 2, but it's worth knowing now

00:02:23.655 --> 00:02:26.536
that “NFT” doesn't always mean a picture.

00:02:26.536 --> 00:02:29.531
Sometimes it's the deed to a real, active position.

00:02:30.831 --> 00:02:34.731
One more category worth knowing: wrapped and receipt tokens.

00:02:34.731 --> 00:02:38.488
WETH wraps ETH into an ERC-20 so it

00:02:38.488 --> 00:02:43.276
behaves like any other token in a contract.

00:02:43.276 --> 00:02:45.851
Lending receipts and LP tokens work the

00:02:45.851 --> 00:02:48.619
same way: they're not the underlying asset

00:02:48.619 --> 00:02:51.129
itself, they're a claim on it, tracked

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:53.811
by a separate token you hold instead.

00:02:53.811 --> 00:02:55.196
That claim is only as good

00:02:55.196 --> 00:02:56.786
as the contract that issued it.

00:02:58.086 --> 00:02:59.586
Approvals and allowances.

00:03:00.886 --> 00:03:03.051
To let a protocol move your tokens

00:03:03.051 --> 00:03:05.463
on your behalf, you approve a spender,

00:03:05.463 --> 00:03:08.185
usually the protocol's own contract, for an

00:03:08.185 --> 00:03:11.021
allowance, an amount of a specific token.

00:03:11.021 --> 00:03:13.501
From that point on, the protocol can pull

00:03:13.501 --> 00:03:16.277
tokens up to that allowance, whenever you act,

00:03:16.277 --> 00:03:19.116
without asking you to sign again each time.

00:03:19.116 --> 00:03:21.406
Read every part before you sign: which

00:03:21.406 --> 00:03:23.461
token, which spender, and how much.

00:03:24.761 --> 00:03:26.664
Here's the trap, and it's the single

00:03:26.664 --> 00:03:29.176
most common one in this lesson's checklist.

00:03:29.176 --> 00:03:33.096
Unlimited approvals are convenient: sign once, and every

00:03:33.096 --> 00:03:36.629
future deposit just works, no repeated prompts.

00:03:36.629 --> 00:03:38.826
But it lets that contract move all of

00:03:38.826 --> 00:03:42.364
that token, forever, not just this transaction's amount.

00:03:42.364 --> 00:03:45.524
If the contract, or the approval mechanism itself,

00:03:45.524 --> 00:03:49.294
is ever exploited, everything you approved is reachable.

00:03:49.294 --> 00:03:52.204
An exact approval costs one extra signature

00:03:52.204 --> 00:03:54.718
later, in exchange for a hard ceiling

00:03:54.718 --> 00:03:57.099
on what any single exploit can take.

00:03:58.399 --> 00:04:02.094
One real number to make this concrete, not hypothetical.

00:04:02.094 --> 00:04:07.500
Blockchain-security researchers have tracked hundreds of millions of dollars

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:11.432
taken by wallet-drainer scams across 2023 and 2024, and

00:04:11.432 --> 00:04:15.434
old, unrevoked approvals are one of their primary tools:

00:04:15.434 --> 00:04:18.874
no new signature needed if a spender contract is

00:04:18.874 --> 00:04:22.404
later found to be malicious or gets exploited.

00:04:22.404 --> 00:04:24.409
This isn't a rare failure mode.

00:04:24.409 --> 00:04:25.944
It's one of the most common

00:04:25.944 --> 00:04:27.479
ones in the entire industry.

00:04:28.779 --> 00:04:31.401
One habit worth naming before the checklist:

00:04:31.401 --> 00:04:33.673
treat “edit allowance” as a button you

00:04:33.673 --> 00:04:35.604
look for, not a rare option.

00:04:35.604 --> 00:04:38.614
Most modern wallets let you change the requested

00:04:38.614 --> 00:04:41.317
amount right in the signing screen, down to

00:04:41.317 --> 00:04:44.074
exactly what this one transaction needs.

00:04:44.074 --> 00:04:46.069
It's not hidden to discourage you.

00:04:46.069 --> 00:04:48.730
It's just easy to miss the first few times,

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:52.419
because unlimited is usually the number a protocol pre-fills.

00:04:53.719 --> 00:04:54.809
Quick check.

00:04:54.809 --> 00:04:58.396
What's the actual risk of leaving an unlimited approval

00:04:58.396 --> 00:05:01.023
in place on a protocol you no longer use?

00:05:05.443 --> 00:05:08.526
The answer: if that contract is ever exploited,

00:05:08.526 --> 00:05:11.159
or the approval itself is abused, it can

00:05:11.159 --> 00:05:13.728
move every unit of that token you hold,

00:05:13.728 --> 00:05:16.233
not just what you originally deposited.

00:05:17.533 --> 00:05:18.878
The gasless signature.

00:05:20.178 --> 00:05:23.503
Here's a detail that catches out even careful people.

00:05:23.503 --> 00:05:27.064
Signature approvals, using a standard called Permit, or

00:05:27.064 --> 00:05:30.243
its successor Permit2, grant an allowance with an

00:05:30.243 --> 00:05:33.658
off-chain signature, not an on-chain transaction.

00:05:33.658 --> 00:05:36.026
There's no gas fee, and often no separate

00:05:36.026 --> 00:05:39.088
confirmation screen with a network warning on it.

00:05:39.088 --> 00:05:41.903
It can feel like a lighter-weight action than it

00:05:41.903 --> 00:05:44.258
is, which is exactly why drainers use it.

00:05:45.558 --> 00:05:47.197
Here's the two side by side,

00:05:47.197 --> 00:05:49.193
so the difference is concrete.

00:05:49.193 --> 00:05:51.972
A normal on-chain approval costs gas, appears

00:05:51.972 --> 00:05:54.147
on an explorer, and usually shows a

00:05:54.147 --> 00:05:56.682
clear network warning in your wallet.

00:05:56.682 --> 00:05:59.903
A Permit signature costs nothing, never touches the

00:05:59.903 --> 00:06:03.000
blockchain until it's actually used, and can look

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:05.282
like a routine “sign in” prompt.

00:06:05.282 --> 00:06:08.043
Read a Permit request exactly as carefully

00:06:08.043 --> 00:06:10.676
as a transaction, every time, because it

00:06:10.676 --> 00:06:12.602
grants exactly the same power.

00:06:13.903 --> 00:06:16.028
So here's the habit for reading any

00:06:16.028 --> 00:06:19.280
signature request, Permit included, in four steps.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:22.465
Stop before signing, even if it looks routine.

00:06:22.465 --> 00:06:26.080
Read what it actually says: token, spender, amount.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:27.818
Ask whether this specific action

00:06:27.818 --> 00:06:29.645
needs that permission at all.

00:06:29.645 --> 00:06:31.990
And only then, sign, or decline.

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:34.178
The whole habit takes maybe ten extra

00:06:34.178 --> 00:06:36.654
seconds, and it's the difference between a

00:06:36.654 --> 00:06:38.900
routine signature and a drained wallet.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:41.290
Quick check.

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:43.855
Why is a gasless Permit signature dangerous?

00:06:48.275 --> 00:06:51.487
The answer: it can grant a token allowance without

00:06:51.487 --> 00:06:54.888
a transaction, so it's easy to sign without noticing,

00:06:54.888 --> 00:06:57.092
and drainers use it to take tokens.

00:06:58.392 --> 00:06:59.397
Worked example.

00:07:00.697 --> 00:07:02.382
One last honest note.

00:07:02.382 --> 00:07:05.080
None of these four checks require you to read

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:08.547
code, or understand what the contract does internally.

00:07:08.547 --> 00:07:11.554
They're all things anyone can verify: an address

00:07:11.554 --> 00:07:14.378
that matches published docs, a number you can

00:07:14.378 --> 00:07:16.894
see and edit, a contract address you can

00:07:16.894 --> 00:07:20.086
compare character by character, and a revoke button

00:07:20.086 --> 00:07:22.411
in a tool built for exactly this.

00:07:22.411 --> 00:07:24.166
The skill isn't technical.

00:07:24.166 --> 00:07:25.926
It's just doing the check, every

00:07:25.926 --> 00:07:27.846
time, instead of trusting the popup.

00:07:29.146 --> 00:07:30.731
Here's the request, exactly as your wallet might

00:07:30.731 --> 00:07:32.551
show it. “Allow 0x3fC9…a1B2 to spend your USDC.

00:07:32.551 --> 00:07:38.271
Amount: Unlimited.” Four checks, in

00:07:38.271 --> 00:07:43.356
order, before you sign anything.

00:07:44.656 --> 00:07:48.216
Check one: is that address the protocol's official router?

00:07:48.216 --> 00:07:50.217
Verify it against the docs or an

00:07:50.217 --> 00:07:52.761
explorer, never just trust the popup.

00:07:52.761 --> 00:07:55.301
Check two: do you actually need unlimited?

00:07:55.301 --> 00:07:57.050
Edit it down to this deposit's

00:07:57.050 --> 00:07:59.156
amount if your wallet allows it.

00:07:59.156 --> 00:08:01.507
Check three: is this actually the

00:08:01.507 --> 00:08:04.486
real USDC contract, not a look-alike?

00:08:04.486 --> 00:08:06.945
And check four, for later: once you're done

00:08:06.945 --> 00:08:09.571
with this protocol, revoke the approval with a

00:08:09.571 --> 00:08:11.471
revoke tool, if you won't be back.

00:08:12.771 --> 00:08:15.449
One more comparison worth carrying with you:

00:08:15.449 --> 00:08:18.426
monthly review versus never reviewing at all.

00:08:18.426 --> 00:08:20.908
A monthly pass through a revoke tool takes

00:08:20.908 --> 00:08:23.275
a few minutes, costs a small network fee

00:08:23.275 --> 00:08:26.161
per revocation, and keeps your live approval list

00:08:26.161 --> 00:08:28.886
matching what you're actually still using.

00:08:28.886 --> 00:08:32.621
Never reviewing means every approval you've ever granted stays

00:08:32.621 --> 00:08:36.237
live indefinitely, on every protocol you've ever tried once,

00:08:36.237 --> 00:08:38.846
whether or not it's still trustworthy today.

00:08:40.146 --> 00:08:41.331
Last check.

00:08:41.331 --> 00:08:43.199
How do you actually know a token

00:08:43.199 --> 00:08:44.727
is the real one, and not a

00:08:44.727 --> 00:08:46.086
fake with the same name?

00:08:50.506 --> 00:08:53.292
The answer: by its contract address, from an

00:08:53.292 --> 00:08:56.759
authoritative source like the project's own docs, never

00:08:56.759 --> 00:08:58.926
by its name or ticker symbol alone.

00:09:00.226 --> 00:09:01.686
Here's your checklist.

00:09:01.686 --> 00:09:03.221
Do it now, for real.

00:09:03.221 --> 00:09:06.666
First, the spender is verified against official docs.

00:09:06.666 --> 00:09:09.721
Second, the allowance is limited to what's needed.

00:09:09.721 --> 00:09:13.901
Third, you read signature requests as carefully as transactions.

00:09:13.901 --> 00:09:16.777
And fourth, you review and revoke approvals on

00:09:16.777 --> 00:09:19.591
a regular schedule, monthly is a good default.

00:09:20.892 --> 00:09:22.137
Let's recap.

00:09:22.137 --> 00:09:23.997
A token's name can be faked;

00:09:23.997 --> 00:09:26.477
only its contract address is real.

00:09:26.477 --> 00:09:29.816
An approval grants a spender an allowance, and unlimited

00:09:29.816 --> 00:09:33.572
approvals let one exploit reach everything you've approved.

00:09:33.572 --> 00:09:36.217
A Permit signature grants the same power

00:09:36.217 --> 00:09:38.668
with no gas and no transaction, which

00:09:38.668 --> 00:09:41.032
is exactly why drainers love it.

00:09:41.032 --> 00:09:43.660
And before signing any approval: verify the

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:46.287
spender, edit the amount, verify the token,

00:09:46.287 --> 00:09:48.497
and revoke it later when you're done.

00:09:49.797 --> 00:09:52.312
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:09:52.312 --> 00:09:54.785
You now have the whole lesson in your

00:09:54.785 --> 00:09:57.975
head: what actually identifies a real token, why

00:09:57.975 --> 00:10:01.099
unlimited approvals are the trap, why a gasless

00:10:01.099 --> 00:10:04.223
Permit deserves the same care as a transaction,

00:10:04.223 --> 00:10:07.127
and how to read a real approval request.

00:10:07.127 --> 00:10:11.152
Next up, Lesson 1.5: Stablecoins and how they break.
